A||N||7||87GTBro||Electrical problems!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||87GTBro||12-23-1999||12:31 AM||||I just purchased an '86 GT<BR>After I got the engine running, it started experiencing interesting electrical problems.  When you are driving the car, no matter what speed, idling or not, the voltage seems to fluctuate.  <BR>I.E.  I was driving at night with headlights on and every time I came to a stop the headlights would dim and  if I turned something else electrical on like the blinkers, the dash lights and headlights would dim and the gauges on the dash would jump.<BR>I could only come to two possible conclusions.  First, the alternator may not be strong enough for the car. (Not enough amps)  Or two, I have a bad ground somewhere.<P>Does anyone know where all the grounding points on the '86 GT are so I can check them?<BR>Thanks<P>P.S. I am a new fiero owner.  It is my first one if my questions seem naive.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-23-1999||02:06 AM||||Also remove the battery cables see if its corroded or the battery terminals, i seen them go jumpy on me before when the terminal just corrode and had to buy a new battery.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-23-1999||02:20 AM||||I would guess, or should I say, start by tightening the alternator belt???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I had some elect problems on my 86GT and asked the same question about grounds? Didn't get much of a response.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>If it is not the alternator, I would start by unpluging the connector in the front compartment, then try everything, Then unplug the connector above the battery, (front half) which controls your tail lights. Then test everything again. What all this un-connecting will do is tell you where your problem is, front end, rear end or cabin.<BR>OH and to answer your question there is one main ground up by the left headlight, one in the console and one above the right rear wheel. (you have to remove the inner fender to get to this one). I am sure there are a bunch more, but that is all that comes to mind at the moment.<BR>Good luck Elect problems are a B$t#@.<BR>I Hate those relays   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-23-1999||07:02 AM||||Welcome to the wonderful world of Fieros and to this Forum in particular.<P>I agree with checking the belt to the alternator, check battery cables for corrosion at the terminals, and check the battery.  The stock alternators are adequate when working properly.  AutoZone will check out the charging system for free while the car sits in their parking lot.<P>There are several grounds in the engine compartment.  If I remember correctly there are a couple right at the base of the battery box and on the right engine lid hinge box.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||12-23-1999||11:56 AM||||Sounds like it could be the "classic" bad ground connetion. Check for loose or corroded connections at the ground braid which is connected between the engine block and the right rear decklid hinge bolt.  Mine needs frequent tightening (exact same simptoms).  A more scientific approach is to measure the voltage between the engine block and the frame of the car.  It should remain quite low.  As you turn on lights, blower, etc., the voltage measurement should remain low.  There is also a ground connection between the battery negative terminal and the chassis.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||12-23-1999||01:28 PM||||I had a problem with some of my gauge readings jumping around and when I checked my grounding points I found that the braided cable had been torn loose from the deck lid in the engine compartment.  I refastened that and everything was ok. ||||reg||12
Z||000006||Raydar||12-23-1999||01:50 PM||||There are also several grounds on the engine block just above the trans. Straight down from the throttle body. Fairly easy to get to, but easy to miss, if you don't know they're there.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula||12-23-1999||04:30 PM||||my battery gauge on the rally pod flucuated right before my alternator went kapultz. The lights never dimmed though.||||reg||1
